00:10:40,980 --> 00:10:48,980
So how does this example differ from the previous case where we do a prisoner's limit followed by coordination game?

261
00:10:48,980 --> 00:10:52,980
The answer is that here, although we only have two games, not 100,

262
00:10:52,980 --> 00:10:55,980
but here in the second game there's two equilibria.

263
00:10:55,980 --> 00:11:01,980
So we can threaten to go and put us into an inferior equilibria in order to get some punishments.

264
00:11:01,980 --> 00:11:06,980
But in this prisoner's limit one, each game only has one equilibria,

265
00:11:06,980 --> 00:11:10,980
and so there doesn't seem like there's anything you can really threaten.

266
00:11:10,980 --> 00:11:13,980
So with two equilibria, we can threaten a funny kind of revenge.

267
00:11:13,980 --> 00:11:16,980
I'm going to send us to the bad equilibria.

268
00:11:16,980 --> 00:11:17,980
It's like, well, why?

269
00:11:17,980 --> 00:11:18,980
Well, like, won't we want to get out?

270
00:11:18,980 --> 00:11:20,980
No, because it's an equilibria.

271
00:11:20,980 --> 00:11:22,980
You know, like, once you're in an equilibria, you don't want to get out of it.

272
00:11:22,980 --> 00:11:23,980
It's the best thing.

273
00:11:23,980 --> 00:11:26,980
Like, at least one person does not want to unilaterally try to get out of it

274
00:11:26,980 --> 00:11:28,980
because then you're even worse off.

275
00:11:28,980 --> 00:11:29,980
Okay.

276
00:11:29,980 --> 00:11:34,980
But if the game is dominant solvable, then there is a second equilibrium out there,

277
00:11:34,980 --> 00:11:37,980
and then there's no threat.

278
00:11:37,980 --> 00:11:41,980
Now, of course, what happens experimentally?

279
00:11:41,980 --> 00:11:45,980
Based upon that rule that your psychology gives you the correct answer, not the game theory?

280
00:11:45,980 --> 00:11:46,980
Yes.

281
00:11:46,980 --> 00:11:51,980
So what do all normal human beings expect will happen if you repeat a prisoner's limit 100 times?

282
00:11:51,980 --> 00:11:54,980
You get a lot of cooperation, and that's what really happens.

283
00:11:54,980 --> 00:11:57,980
And when does the cooperation start breaking down?

284
00:11:57,980 --> 00:12:00,980
Somewhere in the 90s.

285
00:12:00,980 --> 00:12:05,980
So, like, 98, 97, maybe 93 if you're the really paranoid person.

286
00:12:05,980 --> 00:12:08,980
So that's what really happens.

287
00:12:08,980 --> 00:12:09,980
Okay.

288
00:12:09,980 --> 00:12:13,980
So you get more cooperation than you would in a one-shot game,

289
00:12:13,980 --> 00:12:15,980
and in fact you get a lot of cooperation.

290
00:12:15,980 --> 00:12:16,980
All right.

291
00:12:16,980 --> 00:12:17,980
Yes.

292
00:12:17,980 --> 00:12:22,980
So is an appropriate way to kind of think of this as a variation of the centipede game?

293
00:12:22,980 --> 00:12:24,980
The centipede game is very similar.

294
00:12:24,980 --> 00:12:26,980
Yeah, same idea.

295
00:12:26,980 --> 00:12:32,980
Now, there are actually formal models that try to understand why you might get cooperation

296
00:12:32,980 --> 00:12:34,980
if I repeat a prisoner's limit.

297
00:12:34,980 --> 00:12:39,980
So there's even the Nobel Prize winner Reinhard Zeltmann.

298
00:12:39,980 --> 00:12:44,980
Part of what he got, the main thing was actually called the chain store paradox,

299
00:12:44,980 --> 00:12:51,980
and the idea of his model, which can be applied very easily to a prisoner's limit,

300
00:12:51,980 --> 00:13:00,980
is just to say, suppose there's a small probability that your opponent does not have that payoff.

301
00:13:00,980 --> 00:13:04,980
Suppose there's a small probability your opponent doesn't like betraying people.

302
00:13:04,980 --> 00:13:05,980
All right.

303
00:13:05,980 --> 00:13:08,980
Or you could just say, well, there's a small probability the opponent is confused about the game.

304
00:13:08,980 --> 00:13:11,980
That also is another way of interpreting it.

305
00:13:11,980 --> 00:13:12,980
What happens then?

306
00:13:12,980 --> 00:13:15,980
And what Zeltmann proves is that if you have a lot of repetition,

307
00:13:15,980 --> 00:13:19,980
a very small degree of doubt about whether about payoffs

308
00:13:19,980 --> 00:13:24,980
or about whether the other person understands the game can lead to very high levels of cooperation.

309
00:13:24,980 --> 00:13:29,980
And intuitively, if the game is going to go on for 100 turns

310
00:13:29,980 --> 00:13:33,980
and there's a small chance the person will cooperate with you for a long time,

311
00:13:33,980 --> 00:13:37,980
do you want to go and mess it up by immediately betraying him?

312
00:13:37,980 --> 00:13:38,980
No.

313
00:13:38,980 --> 00:13:43,980
You want to say, yeah, maybe there's like a 2% chance this person doesn't understand the game,

314
00:13:43,980 --> 00:13:47,980
so why don't I just play nice and let the game proceed and proceed and proceed,

315
00:13:47,980 --> 00:13:51,980
and then towards the end, then I'll stab him in the back.

316
00:13:51,980 --> 00:13:57,980
And of course, if both sides can have the same shadow of a doubt, the same suspicion,

317
00:13:57,980 --> 00:14:00,980
maybe the other person doesn't understand the game.

318
00:14:00,980 --> 00:14:07,980
And what Zeltmann proves is that as the number of finite repetitions increases,

319
00:14:07,980 --> 00:14:13,980
the amount of doubt you need in order to sustain cooperation for most of the game becomes very small.

320
00:14:13,980 --> 00:14:20,980
So there could be like a 1% chance the other person doesn't understand the game or just has a conscience.

321
00:14:20,980 --> 00:14:27,980
So if there's a 1% chance of either of those things, then even though in fact neither person has a conscience,

322
00:14:27,980 --> 00:14:31,980
that little bit of doubt can lead to cooperation almost to the end.

323
00:14:31,980 --> 00:14:35,980
Now, they still betray in the very last turn or a little bit earlier,

324
00:14:35,980 --> 00:14:39,980
depending upon the exact values of the variables.

325
00:14:39,980 --> 00:14:41,980
But anyway, that's your initial result.

326
00:14:41,980 --> 00:14:45,980
And if you think about, if you happen to be in the real world in this situation,

